are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/97/9752e135eee9c4e585a007f84b0b8b8d220d4e2e1f027fc8a69aebf27066940e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348170 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348170 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79192" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348170 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348170 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6b/6b77b1b8a61b5ec7306fbc0b3d70618e7fdce2237bdcbfd017c23c01b80f2979.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-4,5)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(-1,5)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348170 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c8948ed-478d-597d-8d84-3303f297f4dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348170 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-4,5).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348178 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348178 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79219" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348178 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348178 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348178 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9c6c4b8-31e2-55b5-bdea-fe6d4fa5f314" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348178 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =49{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =49{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =49{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +49{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =49({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =49(1) \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397836 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397836 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106293" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397836 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397836 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d6/d698646baac96e15db7f447c0e1af6dbc0cb03cae50084fbfbb6cab15eabf565.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397836 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da600baa-a9d3-5724-9a5b-8920df8b8600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397836 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=2.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/b7/b76043921a3f9399049d6c3b4f557c7263da4d38a070c66a2dd444dbe2806c8e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397837 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397837 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397837 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397837 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397837 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397837 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397837 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397837 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186397840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397837 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dad99554-fa4d-57b5-9d6d-0148f288e880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397838 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397838 #edb/ref 1082 #e